        <item>
            <title>0.2</title>
            <pubDate>Tue, 01 Sep 2026 07:47:07 -0700</pubDate>
            <sparkle:version>537</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>0.2</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>26.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
            <sparkle:hardwareRequirements>arm64</sparkle:hardwareRequirements>
            <description><![CDATA[<style>
:root { color-scheme: light dark; }
body { font-family: -apple-system, 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif; font-size: 13px; color: #1a1a1a; margin: 12px; }
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { body { color: #e6e6e6; } }
h2 { font-size: 15px; margin: 0 0 8px; } ul { margin: 4px 0 10px; padding-left: 20px; } li { margin-bottom: 3px; }
</style>
<h2>Asobi 0.2 (build 537)</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Web shares are a quarter of the size, and they stream.</b> The review proxy was being encoded at about 26 Mbps for a 1080p clip — 232 MB for a minute of picture, several times what Frame.io or Vimeo send, and more than a normal connection can keep up with. It now encodes in the same band those services use: 5.7 Mbps, 65 MB for that same minute, measured at 0.991 structural similarity to the old encode across the busiest part of the shot. If a job needs the old weight, <code>webshare.bitrateFactor</code> still sets it.</li>
<li><b>The web page buffers like a player instead of a straw.</b> It used to pull three to five seconds of video, one segment at a time, waiting for each. It now pulls twenty seconds at once and quietly caches the whole clip behind the review, so a clip you have watched once plays like a local file.</li>
<li><b>The web review's controls fit a scaled window.</b> On a 14" screen with the window not full — which is what a call looks like — the transport and the markup tools ran off both ends of the bar. The bar wraps now, transport on one row and markup on the next, with nothing off-screen at any width.</li>
<li><b>The web playlist is findable.</b> It was behind an unlabelled button and the letter G. The line in the header that says which clip you're on now opens it.</li>
<li><b>Fixed: a reviewer who can't export could reach Share to Web.</b> Moving it out of a sheet and into the Shared Review panel moved it past the menu's role gating. It follows the same rule as the exports again.</li>
</ul>
]]></description>
